-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.8 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * This computer's DKMS key follows the edition key's design (ORDER 374; ORDER 260, Daniel, 2026-10-25: "we never
    know when the user will migrate the mb or even have to reset it"): DKMS signs the modules it builds on this
    computer (NVIDIA's open modules among them) with this computer's own key, which Secure Boot accepts only once it
    is in the MOK list, and no package enrolls it (Debian #1076269; dkms-project/dkms #429, closed as stale). Its
    certificate (dkms's mok_certificate, /var/lib/dkms/mok.pub by default; DER, a PEM one converted) is kept next to
    every shim as dkms-this-computer.der, and removed there when the computer has no DKMS key, by the postinst, the
    kernel hook, the shim and GRUB triggers and repair, as the edition's is
  * GRUB's menu has a second entry, "Secure Boot: enroll this computer's DKMS key", in the system's language with the
    steps in the nine languages, starting MokManager as the first does ("Enroll key from disk" asks only for a
    confirmation). GRUB looks for the certificate on the partition, by its UUID, when it reads the menu, so the entry
    appears as soon as DKMS has made the key, with no update-grub; update-grub runs once on the upgrade, for the entry
  * sparky-stereo-boot-dkms-key.path places the certificate when DKMS makes or changes the key (the installed
    system's first boot signs the modules its image built unsigned, dkms 3.4.4-1+sparky4), and checks the
    enrolment again when the running kernel's modules.dep is written (depmod, after DKMS installs the modules it
    signed), through sparky-stereo-boot-secureboot.service, which also runs at every boot
  * Under Secure Boot, a DKMS key that is not in shim's MOK list while DKMS modules signed with it are installed for
    the running kernel is said and kept, naming the GRUB entry that enrolls it (no silent fallbacks, Daniel,
    2026-11-01: "either it works or not and the user must know"); it clears at the first boot after the enrolment
  * A DKMS certificate that cannot be written or read is said and kept (esp-dkms-certificate), with
    secureboot.py esp as its repair; the purge removes dkms-this-computer.der from the partition too
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.7 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * GRUB's menu text has one size on every screen, for a PC at a desk (ORDER 361; Daniel, 2026-11-02: "I sit at 50cm
    from the screen, I use it as a monitor, and people installing sparky will be installing on a PC, not in a
    television"): GRUB_STEREO_TEXT_MM (4.2 mm) is no longer enlarged for screens that report a television's size
    (1.2 times the diagonal for a screen over 32 inches); a 43 inch UHD screen gets GRUB's own font instead of 38 px.
    GRUB_STEREO_TEXT_MM and GRUB_FONT set by the person still decide. update-grub runs once on the upgrade, so the
    menu takes the new size
  * The old-kernel cleanup runs at normal priority, as part of the update (ORDER 361; Daniel, 2026-11-02, after "any
    build at update time - DO NEVER CAP EXECUTION": "same with kernel removal"): sparky-stereo-kernel-keep.service
    loses Nice=10
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.6 (2026-11-02)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 350 (Daniel, 2026-11-02: "Why machinery to keep a failure for each?"): the boot's problems are kept
    by the edition's one engine, sparky-stereo-problems, and shown by its session notifier; the boot's own
    store and user units go. status and repair keep working through the engine; dismiss is the engine's Hide
    (yours, kept listed, back when the step fails again); notify starts the session's notifier. The records
    0.1.5 kept in /var/lib/sparky-stereo-boot/problems move into the engine once at the upgrade, keeping when
    each was found. The messages, word for word, are built into journal catalogs; the boot's problems go when
    the package is removed
  * A record of 0.1.5 that cannot move (unreadable, or refused by the engine) stays in the old store, which
    nothing reads any more: it is said once and kept as the boot problem old-store, which names the store and
    each record with what failed, so the next login tells it; repair tries the move again and the problem is
    over when every record moved. The package still configures
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.5 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Nothing fails silently (ORDER 345; Daniel, 2026-11-01: "either it works or not and the user must know"):
    a step of the boot's upkeep that cannot do what it is for says what failed, why and what to do in the
    system's language, keeps it with its command, exit status and output in /var/lib/sparky-stereo-boot/problems
    until the step succeeds again (lib/problems.py), and the session shows it as a notification;
    sparky-stereo-boot status lists the problems, repair runs again what each names, dismiss hides one
  * postinst: a failed update-grub fails the package, as grub2's postinst does; systemd's reload, the units'
    start and the certificate on the EFI system partition are said and kept, and the package configures;
    update-grub runs once on the upgrade from 0.1.4, for GRUB's new record
  * The kernel hooks say and keep a certificate that cannot be placed and a cleanup that cannot be queued;
    the kernel still installs and zz-update-grub still runs after them
  * secureboot.py: an EFI system partition named in /etc/fstab but not mounted, a missing certificate and a
    failed copy are problems; an EFI system partition without shim is said
  * Removal: failed grub-editenv, rm, update-initramfs and, on purge, update-grub fail the removal, with the
    messages in the nine languages written into the postrm
  * Kernel cleanup: update-initramfs and update-grub after a purge are checked; a failed purge, apt's simulation
    naming more than the plan and any unexpected error make the service fail and are kept (--now runs the
    cleanup without a queued install, for repair)
  * The 3D splash's hand-over: a failed deactivate, hand-over service or plymouth quit, a login screen that does
    not start and no picture before the limit quit the splash, keep the reason and fail the unit; with no
    recorded splash picture it no longer waits ten minutes; plymouth-quit.service fails when a running splash
    cannot be quit (Debian's unit ignores that)
  * GRUB: update-grub's output names the resolution GRUB is given and the ones it takes when the firmware does
    not offer it; no connected screen with an EDID and a font that cannot be made are kept, with grub-mkfont's
    error; grub-display records what GRUB was given, and sparky-stereo-boot-check.service compares it at boot
    with the resolution the kernel got from GRUB
  * 46 new messages, in en, pl, pt_BR, fr, de, ja, ko, zh_CN and zh_TW
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.4 (2026-10-30)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Two kernels per flavour (ORDER 298): after a new kernel has installed (its image, its boot image and its
    GRUB entry written), each flavour keeps the running kernel and the newest, or the one before the newest when
    that is the running one; the others are purged, all their packages (image, binary, modules, headers, the
    per-release base, their -dbg and Lean Code companions, and the kbuild and headers-common packages nothing
    else needs), then their module folders and DKMS records, and every kept kernel's boot image and GRUB's menu
    are written again. Never a general autoremove: only the kernels' own packages, checked against apt's
    simulation first. A kernel hook records the install and sparky-stereo-kernel-keep.service does the work
    once apt and dpkg are done; nothing happens in a live session, a chroot or a container
  * Every boot image, always: update_initramfs=all, so every update-initramfs -u (what Plymouth's, the
    firmware's and the edition's triggers run) writes the boot image of every installed kernel; initramfs-tools'
    update-initramfs.conf is kept as update-initramfs.conf.debian and comes back on removal
  * kernel-keep.py --dry-run prints the plan
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.3 (2026-10-25)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Secure Boot without the edition's key is never a dead end: a new or reset motherboard, or Secure Boot turned on
    after the installation, leaves the firmware's MOK list without the Sparky Stereo OS key and GRUB refuses the
    edition's kernel. The package ships the edition's public certificate and keeps it next to every shim on the
    EFI system partition (at install, after every kernel, and whenever shim's or GRUB's signed programs are
    installed again); GRUB's menu gets "Secure Boot: enroll the Sparky Stereo OS key" in the system's language,
    which shows the steps in the nine required languages and starts the MokManager beside GRUB; under Secure Boot
    the menu asks shim about the newest kernel first, and when shim refuses it the enrolment entry is the default,
    so the timeout starts it
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.2 (2026-10-24)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * On 32-bit UEFI, nokaslr only while a kernel in /boot lacks the fix: the edition's kernels from build 12 on
    (7.3.0-rc6-stereo3d12) keep themselves below 4 GiB there and boot with KASLR; Debian's kernel and older
    builds still get nokaslr, as GRUB gives every kernel in its menu the same command line
  * nokaslr goes into GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX, so the recovery entries get it too (0.1.1 left them without it)
  * SPARKY_STEREO_IA32_NOKASLR: auto (that rule), yes (always on 32-bit UEFI) or no (never)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.1 (2026-10-16)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * NVIDIA's display driver in the boot image, so the boot splash runs on an NVIDIA screen: nvidia-drm with
    nvidia-modeset, nvidia and their firmware, loaded by udev for an NVIDIA display device; modeset and fbdev on
  * SPARKY_STEREO_NVIDIA_EARLY: auto (an NVIDIA display device, or a live system), yes or no; a system that
    turned NVIDIA's driver off keeps it out of the boot image too
  * On 32-bit UEFI (EFI mixed mode) the kernel starts with nokaslr: at a randomized physical address Linux maps
    its firmware-call return trampoline without execute permission and stops at the first firmware call
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.0 (2026-10-16)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * First version: the saved choice of the main screen exported for the boot
  * GRUB at the main screen's resolution, kept through the kernel
  * The boot splash in the saved 3D mode or stereo layout (plymouth.stereo=)
  * The login screen gets the display settings (Plasma Login Manager, SDDM)
  * The splash handed over to the login screen with no new mode set
